    ("The 'New Public Administration' (NPA) movement emerged from which landmark academic conference?", "The Minnowbrook Conference I (1968) chaired by Dwight Waldo", ["The Bretton Woods Conference (1944)", "The San Francisco UN Conference (1945)", "The Hague Peace Conference (1899)"], "Young administrative scholars rejected value-free positivism, demanding Relevance, Values, Social Equity, and Change."),
    ("What were the four core thematic pillars championed by New Public Administration (NPA)?", "Relevance, Values, Social Equity, and Change", ["Economy, Efficiency, Hierarchy, and Control", "Profits, Competition, Deregulation, and Exports", "Secrecy, Neutrality, Uniformity, and Stability"], "NPA challenged public administration to act as proactive agents for social justice and equitable distribution."),
    ("David Osborne and Ted Gaebler's book 'Reinventing Government' (1992) laid the foundations for:", "'New Public Management' (NPM) - 'Steering rather than Rowing'", ["Scientific Management and Taylorism", "Classical Max Weberian Bureaucracy", "Marxist State Collectivism"], "NPM introduced entrepreneurial government, competition, customer focus, performance contracting, and decentralization."),
