admin_hard = [
    ("Herbert Simon's logical positivism in 'Administrative Behavior' drew a sharp methodological boundary between:", "'Fact' propositions (subject to empirical verification) and 'Value' propositions (ethical/normative choices)", ["Private profit corporations and Public government ministries", "Written formal rules and Unwritten informal customs", "Civilian bureaucrats and Uniformed military officers"], "Simon argued administrative science can examine only fact propositions; value choices belong to politics."),
    ("Mary Parker Follett's concept of 'Constructive Conflict' in organizational dynamics advocated resolving disputes via:", "Integration (inventing a creative solution meeting the underlying desires of both sides without compromise)", ["Dominance (one party crushing the opponent completely)", "Compromise (both parties sacrificing essential goals)", "Avoidance (ignoring the existence of conflict)"], "Follett argued integration creates emergent, lasting solutions without leaving resentful residual compromises."),
    ("Mary Parker Follett's 'Law of the Situation' in organizational leadership dictates that:", "Orders should be dictated by the objective facts of the situation, not by the personal power of the boss", ["Leaders should invent arbitrary commands to test loyalty", "Subordinates should ignore all situational variables", "External political parties should dictate office procedures"], "Follett advocated 'Power-with' rather than 'Power-over', making authority functional and situation-driven."),
    ("Chester Barnard's 'Contribution-Satisfaction Equilibrium' in executive governance posits that an organization survives only if:", "The inducements/satisfactions offered to participants equal or exceed the contributions demanded from them", ["The organization extracts 100% surplus labor from workers without pay", "Profits are distributed solely to external investors", "The state nationalizes all physical assets of the firm"], "Barnard proved organizations collapse when participants perceive contributions outweigh the psychological rewards."),
    ("In ecological public administration, Fred Riggs' 'Bazaar-Canteen Model' describes economic price determination in prismatic societies as:", "Indeterminate price indeterminacy, price bargaining, and subsidized canteens for the politically privileged", ["Perfect market competition under Adam Smith's invisible hand", "Total centralized mathematical Gosplan state pricing", "Purely barter exchange without monetary currency"], "Prices fluctuate based on family connections, ethnic status, and political bribery rather than pure market supply and demand."),
    ("Philip Selznick's landmark organizational study 'TVA and the Grass Roots' (1949) analyzed how public agencies survive through:", "'Co-optation' (absorbing external hostile elements into policy-making to avert threats to stability)", ["Pure military coercion against local farmers", "Complete isolation from local agricultural communities", "Immediate surrender of all statutory powers"], "The Tennessee Valley Authority compromised its progressive forestry goals by co-opting conservative local farm bureaus."),
    ("Michel Crozier's sociological study 'The Bureaucratic Phenomenon' (1964) analyzed the French bureaucracy as an organization that:", "'Cannot correct its behavior by learning from its errors', where power rests in the control of uncertainty", ["Instantly adapts to technological disruptions with agility", "Eliminates all internal interpersonal rivalry", "Operates entirely without any written paperwork"], "Crozier demonstrated that groups controlling remaining zones of uncertainty wield informal power over rules."),
    ("Christopher Hood's classic public administration doctrine classifies New Public Management (NPM) into doctrines emphasizing:", "Hands-on professional management, explicit standards, output controls, and unbundling of units", ["Total state monopoly, lifelong tenure, secrecy, and input budgets", "Feudal patronage, arbitrary discretion, and oral commands", "Religious orthodoxy and ecclesiastical tribunals"], "Hood distilled NPM into seven doctrines emphasizing market discipline, disaggregation, and private-style management."),
    ("Christopher Hood's 'Grid-Group Cultural Theory' of administrative control classifies organizational control into four types:", "Hierarchist, Egalitarian, Individualist, and Fatalist styles of administrative control", ["Monarchical, Republican, Anarchic, and Totalitarian", "Alpha, Beta, Gamma, and Delta accounting mechanisms", "Civilian, Military, Judicial, and Diplomatic cadres"], "Control styles depend on strength of group boundaries (group) and number of externally imposed prescriptions (grid)."),
    ("Patrick Dunleavy's post-NPM concept of 'Digital-Era Governance' (DEG) argues modern public administration is defined by:", "Reintegration, Needs-based holism, and Digitally enabled administrative transformation", ["Complete privatization of all public sector infrastructure", "Returning to 19th-century manual paper file registries", "Abolishing all digital internet technologies in government"], "DEG moves past NPM fragmentation by reintegrating services around citizen life events using digital automation."),
    ("Pollitt and Bouckaert's 'Neo-Weberian State' (NWS) model describes continental European administrative modernization that:", "Reaffirms the central steering role of the state and administrative law while modernizing service responsiveness", ["Abandons all constitutional law in favor of unconstrained free markets", "Eliminates civil service tenure and establishes political spoils", "Restores absolute hereditary monarchical governance"], "NWS retains administrative legality, rule-bound neutrality, and civil service status while modernizing procedures."),
    ("In Anthony Downs' formal bureaucratic typology in 'Inside Bureaucracy' (1967), 'Climbers' and 'Conservers' are motivated by:", "Climbers seek power and rapid promotion; Conservers seek security, stability, and retention of existing perquisites", ["Both seek only ideological policy revolutions", "Both refuse all monetary salary increases", "Both seek immediate early resignation from government"], "Downs analyzed how self-interest shapes bureaucratic career strategies, risk aversion, and institutional rigidity."),
    ("William Niskanen's formal economic model of bureaucracy posits that a bureau is a monopoly supplier facing:", "A sponsor (legislature) with a bilateral monopoly, enabling the bureau to maximize its total budget allocation", ["Thousands of competing commercial retail shops", "Zero political or legislative supervision", "A perfectly competitive market pricing mechanism"], "Because sponsors lack cost information, bureaus produce beyond social optimum, extracting all consumer surplus."),
    ("Barry Bozeman's 'Publicness Theory' in public administration argues that:", "All organizations are public in varying degrees of political and economic authority influence", ["Only government ministries are public; corporations are 100% private", "Publicness is determined solely by the number of office desks", "Public organizations are identical to private profit cartels"], "Bozeman rejected simplistic public-private dichotomies, measuring publicness by exposure to political constraints."),
    ("In public accountability literature, the 'Accountability Paradox' in autonomous arm's-length agencies describes:", "Greater formal autonomy designed to insulate regulators often results in ambiguous democratic accountability", ["Agencies having zero statutory authority of any kind", "Courts taking over day-to-day administrative management", "Citizens electing all regulatory directors directly"], "Arm's-length agencification shields technocrats from partisan interference but muddles political accountability."),
    ("The 'Nolan Committee on Standards in Public Life' (1995) established the foundational 'Seven Principles of Public Life':", "Selflessness, Integrity, Objectivity, Accountability, Openness, Honesty, and Leadership", ["Power, Profit, Secrecy, Loyalty, Compliance, Speed, and Caution", "Hierarchy, Discretion, Deference, Privilege, Seniority, and Caution", "Wealth, Influence, Patronage, Nepotism, and Status"], "Lord Nolan's principles serve as the international ethical bedrock for ministerial and civil service codes of conduct."),
    ("In public administration ethics, 'Administrative Evil' (Adams and Balfour) describes the historical phenomenon where:", "Civil servants engage in devastating destructive acts while diligently performing routine technical duties", ["Bureaucrats steal small amounts of stationery supplies", "Ministers accept campaign donations legally", "Courts issue stays on administrative transfers"], "Moral inversion masks horrific administrative harm as ordinary, efficient, and professional bureaucratic duty."),
    ("The 'Coasean Theory of the Firm' applied to public bureaucracy by Oliver Williamson explains that government internalizes functions when:", "Asset specificity is high, uncertainty is profound, and market contracting transaction costs are exorbitant", ["Market contracting costs are absolute zero", "Private vendors provide free services out of charity", "The legislature passes an annual deficit budget"], "High opportunism and asset-specific investments make direct public bureaucratic hierarchical governance more efficient than outsourcing."),
    ("In street-level administrative discretion, the 'Creaming' or 'Skimming' phenomenon refers to caseworkers:", "Selecting the easiest, most cooperative clients to boost performance metrics while neglecting the hardest cases", ["Stealing public welfare budget funds directly", "Distributing dairy milk subsidies to farmers", "Failing to complete annual property tax filings"], "Under quantitative target pressures, caseworkers pick clients closest to self-sufficiency to inflate success rates."),
    ("In development administration, Robert Chambers' 'Participatory Rural Appraisal' (PRA) revolutionized methodology by:", "Reversing top-down expert dominance and empowering rural villagers to map, analyze, and plan their own development", ["Relying exclusively on satellite imagery without visiting villages", "Imposing central World Bank econometric models on tribes", "Transferring all agricultural lands to urban corporations"], "PRA demands 'handing over the stick' so local subaltern communities express and own their local reality."),
    ("The 'Dark Side of Organization' analyzed by Charles Perrow in 'Normal Accidents' (1984) demonstrates that:", "In high-risk complex technological systems with interactive complexity and tight coupling, catastrophic accidents are inevitable", ["Accidents are always caused solely by lazy operator negligence", "Complex engineering systems never experience failures", "Modern computers eliminate all physical mechanical faults"], "Tight coupling and unanticipated non-linear feedback make catastrophic failures structurally built into the system."),
    ("The 'Whole-of-Government' (Joined-Up Government) administrative approach emerged primarily to resolve:", "Inter-agency fragmentation, departmental silos, and lack of coordination across complex wicked problems", ["Excessive profitability of state-owned enterprises", "Shortages of office furniture in district secretariats", "The complete absence of written administrative laws"], "Joined-up governance coordinates across ministries to tackle cross-cutting challenges like national security and climate."),
    ("In public-private partnership (PPP) governance, Oliver Hart's 'Incomplete Contracts Theory' shows that:", "Contracts cannot foresee all future contingencies, creating severe renegotiation hold-up risks and quality shading", ["Contracts are always 100% comprehensive and cover all future events", "Private contractors never seek to maximize their profits", "Government concession agreements never require renegotiation"], "When quality aspects cannot be fully contracted, private concessionaires have incentives to cut corners on unmeasured quality."),
    ("The administrative doctrine of 'Proportionality' applied in judicial review of administrative action requires that:", "The measure taken must be suitable, necessary, and balanced, imposing the least restrictive burden to achieve the goal", ["All administrative punishments must be identical regardless of offense", "Courts must substitute their own wisdom for executive choice", "Every government action requires a nationwide referendum"], "Proportionality balances the intensity of state interference against the legitimate public objective sought."),
    ("In public expenditure management, 'Fiscal Illusion' (Amilcare Puviani, James Buchanan) posits that governments:", "Design complex, indirect, and hidden tax structures to make taxpayers underestimate the true cost of public services", ["Publish simplified single-page tax returns for all citizens", "Refuse to collect any revenue and rely on printing currency", "Inform citizens transparently of exact costs daily"], "Hidden withholding and indirect taxes obscure the tax burden, enabling public sector expansion beyond voter desires."),
    ("The 'Iron Triangle' in policy and administrative subsystems describes a durable, self-serving policy alliance among:", "Executive Bureaucrats, Legislative Committees, and Organized Commercial Interest Groups", ["The President, Supreme Court, and Armed Forces", "Print Media, Television Channels, and Advertising Firms", "University Academics, School Teachers, and Students"], "Iron triangles form insulated policy sub-governments that trade mutual favors and resist external democratic scrutiny."),
    ("In public administration, 'Regulatory Guillotine' methodology developed by Peco Hinrichsen differs from routine legal review by:", "Reversing the burden of proof: all regulations are eliminated automatically unless explicitly justified as essential", ["Requiring judges to examine each regulation over 20 years", "Imposing heavy personal fines on citizens who read statutes", "Delegating regulatory drafting exclusively to labor unions"], "Ministries must defend regulations on a fast-track clock against criteria of legality and economic necessity."),
    ("The 'Principal-Agent-Client' triadic model in social welfare administration analyzes the conflicting pressures on frontline staff between:", "Strict upward accountability to managers (principal) vs empathetic downward responsiveness to needy clients", ["Complying with foreign embassy guidelines vs local traffic laws", "Maximizing commercial corporate profits vs executive stock grants", "Managing personal investment portfolios vs official filing"], "Caseworkers negotiate tensions between restrictive bureaucratic rules and the visceral, urgent human needs of clients."),
    ("The 'Public Trust Doctrine' in environmental administration holds that certain natural resources (rivers, air, seashores) are:", "Held by the sovereign state in trust for the free, unimpeded use of the general public and cannot be privatized", ["The absolute private commercial property of the ruling prime minister", "Available for unlimited commercial auction to the highest bidder", "Completely unmanageable by any government body"], "Supreme Court applied this in the M.C. Mehta Kamal Nath case, barring commercial alienation of riverbeds."),
    ("The 'Wednesbury Unreasonableness' standard of judicial review established in 1948 holds that a court may intervene only if an administrative decision is:", "So utterly unreasonable and perverse that no sensible authority could ever have conceived of making it", ["Slightly inconvenient to a commercial business interest", "Disapproved of by editorial columns in national newspapers", "Adopted by a simple legislative majority rather than two-thirds"], "Associated Provincial Picture Houses v. Wednesbury Corp established a high threshold of irrationality for judicial intervention."),
    ("The administrative concept of 'Regulatory Co-Production' (Elinor Ostrom) highlights that public services are effectively produced when:", "Citizens and community users actively contribute inputs alongside formal state administrative professionals", ["The state eliminates all citizen involvement in governance", "Private corporations replace 100% of municipal public workers", "Robotic artificial intelligence replaces human judges"], "Education and policing succeed only when students, parents, and community members co-produce safety and learning."),
    ("In civil service ethics, the concept of 'Noble Cause Corruption' occurs when public administrators or police officers:", "Commit procedural or legal violations believing the noble righteous end justifies illegitimate corrupt means", ["Accept small monetary bribes for issuing routine certificates", "Leisurely take long lunch breaks during working hours", "Refuse to run for elected political office"], "Officers falsifying evidence to convict a guilty suspect represent dangerous noble cause corruption undermining rule of law."),
    ("The 'Administrative Law Judge' (ALJ) system in the United States and Administrative Tribunals in India under Article 323A are designed to:", "Provide specialized, expeditious adjudication of administrative disputes outside ordinary slow civil courts", ["Abolish the Supreme Court's constitutional writ jurisdiction", "Allow civil servants to decide their own criminal murder cases", "Privatize the national judicial appointment commission"], "Tribunals provide specialized administrative and service dispute resolution while remaining subject to High Court review.")
]
